 all three nodes, availability was directly shown to persist with one node down and a write accepted and replicated during that window, and automatic IST-based resynchronization was directly observed when the node returned. The independent provider-wide node-restart protocol was completed for all three nodes with matching pre/post boot-identity, restart acknowledgement, and full cluster/data recovery evidence, cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots. Operational hygiene is good: package/apt-repo swaps and mysqld_safe recovery sessions were all purposeful, bounded, and left no residue or broken state in the after-executor snapshot.","limitations":["The 'availability during disruption' and 'auto-resync' tests used systemctl stop/start of the mariadb service rather than a full node reboot; this is a valid and common way to exercise Galera's node-loss/rejoin behavior but is a narrower disruption than an OS-level failure.","bind-address=0.0.0.0 was applied on all three nodes without additional evidence of network-level access restriction beyond the managed topology; this is standard for Galera cluster communication but was not independently verified as scoped to only the cluster's private network."]},"ttft_ms":29329,"type":"result","duration_ms":80915,"uuid":"73b3aa83-6026-44c8-baa6-b7b8892f9bf4","ttft_stream_ms":3673,"time_to_request_ms":41,"first_content_frame_ms":4007,"queued_turn_count":0}
